e style bias persists even in pairs where content shifts, which points to style being the dominant factor, though it doesn't conclusively isolate whether content changes amplify the bias.\n\nNow looking at the cross-axis gradient across the completed experiments: verbosity shows 14% NLI rejection with 77.3% BT, formality has 12% rejection at 66.5% BT, and register comes in at 6% rejection with 55.3% BT. I need to verify whether register has a placebo correction since the placebo was only run for GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B, and the register BT comes from Qwen3-32B. The qualitative argument hinges on how NLI rejection correlates with the manipulation effect.\n\nThe key insight is that higher manipulation invasiveness tracks with higher BT, which contradicts the idea of uniform format amplification. Even after applying placebo correction—where verbosity drops to ~68%, formality to ~58%, and register to ~47%—the gradient persists across axes. This 20+ percentage point spread from verbosity to register suggests axis-specific style detection beyond just the amplification floor, which would be the case if all corrected BTs converged to similar values. Under a worst-case scenario where the placebo shows ~10pp amplification, the argument weakens but doesn't collapse because the gradient itself survives correction.\n\nI should also run through the remaining planned experiments, starting with the multi-judge formality pairwise test where Gemini-2.0-Flash is expected to show around 59% BT.\n\nLooking at the Gemini results, the formality bias is moderate with a confidence interval spanning 0.515 to 0.665 and p=0.025, while the Likert scale shows essentially no effect. This complicates the clean 2/4 split I was working with, but it actually supports a more nuanced narrative about architecture-dependent susceptibility—a spectrum where GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B show strong bias, Gemini sits in the middle, and Claude and Llama show none. Though I need to check whether Gemini was even part of t