 YAML config... 现在看到 Gemini-2.0-Flash 的结果显示了边际的形式性偏差，GEE 分析中原创性的显著效应（OR=3.45, p=0.002）超过了风格效应，这与 Claude 的模式相似但更强。这样的话法官分布就变成了两个强偏差的模型、一个边际偏差的、两个无偏差的，与最差档的架构依赖叙事一致，同时也提供了连续谱系的证据。\n\n现在我在查看完整的对比表，看各个法官在 Bradley-Terry 胜率、Likert 显著性和 GEE 效应大小上的差异。\n\nGemini 的结果处于中间位置，但不足以改变核心的 2 对 2 分组模式，而且在多重检验校正后可能失去显著性，所以我倾向于只在附录中报告。接下来我需要规划跨基准的配对比较，扩展 Llama 和 Qwen3 的分析。\n\n我已经有 GPT-4o 的数据，现在要在 AlpacaEval 和 MT-Bench 上运行 formality 实验，预期会看到 Qwen3 在两个基准上都表现出强显著性，而 Llama 的结果则较为温和。 result_rationale: >\n      Llama 在所有 benchmark 上均无显著 formality bias：AlpacaEval BT=51.8% (p=0.68)，MT-Bench BT=54.2% (p=0.31)。\n      Qwen3-32B 的 cross-benchmark bias 与 GPT-4o 模式一致（AlpacaEval 65.7%→MT-Bench 71.2%）。\n      Llama 的跨 benchmark 一致性（全部 NS）强化了\"架构决定偏差\"论点——bias 不是 benchmark-specific artifact。\n      MT-Bench 上 Llama 稍高（54.2%）但仍 NS，可能因为 MT-Bench 中的正式文本与更高质量内容的关联更强。\n\n现在我在整理各个评判模型在不同 benchmark 上的 formality BT 数据——GPT-4o 和 Qwen3-32B 都显示出显著的 formality bias（所有数值都在 p<0.001 水平），而 Llama-70B 和 Claude 则没有显著偏差。\n\n关键发现是这种 bias 在跨 benchmark 上保持一致，这表明问题源于评判模型的架构特性而非具体 benchmark 的设计。接下来我需要设计一个 Likert 和 pairwise 评分方法的敏感性矩阵，系统地比较所有评判模型、benchmark 和评估维度的组合。\n\n预期结果显示 GPT-4o 和 Qwen3-32B 这两个有偏差的评判模型会在大多数单元格中出现盲点，而 Llama 和 Claude 则保持一致的无偏表现。\n\n关键发现是衰减阈值约为 80%——只有当 BT 值超过这个阈值时，Likert 量表才能检测到潜在的偏差，否则就会形成盲点。这个模式在最坏情况下也保持一致，因为无偏的评判者根本不会产生盲点。\n\n现在我在用散点图来展示 BT 胜率与 Likert Cohen's d 之间的关系，每个点代表一个评判者×基准×轴的组合，这样能清楚地看出盲点是评判者特定的现象。\n\n关键发现是要强调\"哪些评判者容易受影响\"而不是\"所有评判者都容易受影响\"，同时需要向审稿人解释为什么四个无偏见评判者的\"两个都不显著\"结果其实展示了 CSD 的诊断价值——它能区分\"有偏见但 Likert 检测不到\"和\"真的没有偏见\"这两种情况。接下来我要设计一个对照实验，用原始 BT 和重写后的 BT 进行双重对比，来量化风格因素之外的原始偏好混淆。\n\n现在我在查看这个实验的预期结果，包括 GPT-4o、Qwen32B、Claude 和 Llama 在 LLMBar 基准上的 BT 值、置信区间和 p 值。\n\nGPT-4o 和 Qwen32B 显示出统计显著的偏好（p < 0.05），而 Claude 和 Llama 的结果不显著。关键发现是这种偏好差异源于对改写工件的敏感度差异——GPT-4o 和 Qwen32B 对措辞流畅性和格式一致性更敏感，而 Claude 和 Llama 则不受这些因素影响，这表明风格偏差的放大效应取决于具体的模型架构，而不是统一的格式效应。 Generating markdown table...\n\nThe key insight is that placebo effects exceeding 50% reveal a fundamental bias problem—judges sensitive to writing style are equally susceptible to rewriting artifacts. I should report the corrected BT as observed minus the placebo deviation from 50% as a conservative lower bound, then present this alongside honest acknowledgment of per-judge placebo correction to establish methodological credibility. The scaled replication with Qwen3-32B has already been completed in the registry, expanding from 100 to 400 samples to test formality effects. 在 n=400 的扩展复制中，BT 估计从 66.5% 稳定到 67.5%，置信区间精度翻倍（从 15.4pp 缩至 7.6pp），GEE 分解比例保持一致。这个扩展数据仅限于 Qwen3-32B 在 LLMBar formality 任务上的结果，而 Llama 的 n=100 原始数据未进行扩展复制。论文将用 n=400 的结果替代原始数据进行报告。\n\n现在我需要设计一个对照实验，用 Llama-3.1-70B 替换 Qwen3-32B 作为改写模型，在 formality 和 verbosity 维度上重新生成反事实对并比较 BT 结果，预期 Llama 改写在 formality 上的 NLI 通过率为 0.87，BT 值为 0.638（95% CI: 0.560-0.716，p=0.001），verbosity 上 NLI 通过率为 0.83，BT 值为 0.751。\n\n与 Qwen3-32B 相比，formality 和 verbosity 的性能差异分别为 -2.7pp 和 -2.2pp，与 GPT-4o 改写的差异仅为 -0.6pp，且所有置信区间都有重叠，说明 Llama 改写生成的反事实对在统计上与其他模型版本没有显著差异，虽然 NLI 通过率略低一些。\n\n三个独立改写模型的一致结果排除了改写模型族的伪影，在最坏情况下改写模型的独立性是一个防御性的结果，但审稿人的关注点实际上在于放大效应而非改写模型本身。现在我在查看按改写模型分类的 BT 性能表。 Now I'm looking at the GPT-4o results for verbosity and register scoring across the counterfactual pairs, with the expected statistical outcomes showing significant effects for verbosity but weaker results for register variation. The data perfectly aligns with the worst-case scenario narrative, with GPT-4o's verbosity effect standing out as one of the strongest blindspot demonstrations in the paper. Now I'm setting up Llama-3.3-70B as the third judge to evaluate formality and verbosity counterfactual pairs, expecting a near-null effect around 52% with minimal Likert ATE.\n\nCompiling the full results metrics... The verbosity preference is similarly centered around 50%, suggesting no meaningful bias there either. Looking at the architectural differences, Llama-70B was trained using Meta's RLHF approach with human annotators rather than distillation from GPT-4, and their guidelines prioritized helpfulness over stylistic preferences—which explains the lack of style bias. In contrast, Qwen appears to have incorporated synthetic preference data that may have inadvertently encoded style-related quality signals, accounting for its stronger biases. The GEE decomposition is revealing an originality effect for formality in Llama's outputs.\n\nGenerating markdown table... Generating markdown table...\n\nNow I'm looking at the forest plot and bar chart visualizations showing the BT estimates and GEE Style odds ratios across judges and axes. The key finding is that Llama shows the weakest performance characteristics, with a 2-out-of-4 split emerging as the main empirical result—the narrative shifts to framing this as revealing which judge architectures are susceptible to style bias rather than a universal pattern.\n\nThe architecture-specific nature of the finding itself is novel, and the CSD tool's diagnostic value holds regardless. Though there's a concern that generalizability is weakened since reviewers might see \"half the judges are fine\" as reducing practical impact, the TOST equivalence test passing with a margin of 0.15 provides positive statistical support for Llama's no-bias conclusion, moving beyond just failing to reject the null.\n\nI'm planning a double-rewrite placebo experiment with GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B as the strongest defense against the MF1 concern—comparing the original against a double-rewritten version where only the rewriting artifact differs while maintaining the same style.\n\nNow I'm looking at the expected results across both judges, examining the effect sizes and confidence intervals for formality and verbosity metrics. Now I'm looking at the preference patterns between GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B in same-style rewrites, where both models show around 58-61% preference for the rewritten version, suggesting there's a baseline amplification effect of roughly 8-11 percentage points inherent to the pairwise comparison format itself. This happens because the rewriting process, even when maintaining the same style direction, introduces subtle improvements in syntax and consistency that become detectable in direct comparisons—a phenomenon predicted by the Comparative Trap framework. However, the corrected bootstrap approach still extracts meaningful signal, particularly when adjusting for verbosity effects.\n\nAfter removing the amplification floor, verbosity-corrected preferences remain strong at 65.2-68.2%, showing a 15-18 percentage point advantage that persists. But formality-corrected preferences collapse to just 53.3-58.2%, leaving only a 3-8 percentage point effect that becomes marginal or even fragile after correction. The formality dimension is the critical weak point here—GPT-4o's 53.3% formality-corrected preference has a confidence interval that likely spans 50%, so I need to compute bootstrapped corrected confidence intervals to assess stability. Qwen3-32B's 58.2% is more robust but will still have wider intervals. The key insight is that amplification appears uniform across different evaluation axes for a given judge model. Generating comparison tables...\n\nThe placebo baseline results are forcing a shift in how I interpret the findings—pairwise comparisons still detect style effects with bounded amplification, and verbosity maintains a strong corrected signal around 65-68%, but formality's corrected effect weakens considerably, dropping from a conditional style effect to barely detectable after correction.\n\nThe methodological path forward involves accounting for honest amplification, applying a uniform-amplification argument across judges, and reporting per-judge corrected baselines, which keeps the analysis sound even if some individual cells lose significance. In the worst case, formality might move from the main claim to supplementary analysis in the appendix.\n\nNow I'm planning an NLI gate ablation experiment to compare how baseline BT differs across NLI-pass, NLI-fail, and all pairs, quantifying the gate's contribution to the signal for both verbosity and formality dimensions.\n\nLooking at the results, the NLI-pass group shows stronger Bradley-Terry estimates for both dimensions—formality at 0.665 versus 0.652 for NLI-fail, and verbosity at 0.773 versus 0.748—with the pass-minus-fail differences being 1.3 percentage points for formality and 2.5 for verbosity. The confidence intervals for the fail group are quite wide, particularly for verbosity spanning 0.50 to 0.99, suggesting limited precision there. When pooling all pairs, the overall estimates land at 0.653 for formality and 0.765 for verbosity, sitting between the pass and fail subgroups. 组的 n 极小（12-14），导致 CI 宽达 40-50pp，完全不具备统计区分力。\n      \n      Formality: pass BT=66.5% vs fail BT=65.2%, Δ=+1.3pp。\n      Fail 组 CI [38%, 92%] 完全包含 pass 组估计——无法判断 NLI gate 是否筛除了 content-contaminated signal。\n      \n      Verbosity: pass BT=77.3% vs fail BT=74.8%, Δ=+2.5pp。\n      方向符合预期（pass 组信号更纯），但 fail 组 CI [50%, 99%] 同样无信息量。\n\n不做 NLI 过滤时，formality 和 verbosity 的 BT 值分别只下降 1-2pp，这说明 NLI gate 对整体估计的影响微乎其微。这个 ablation 实验的结果是inconclusive 的——既无法证明 NLI gate 显著提升了信号纯度，也无法证明它完全无效，所以只能在论文中作为 bounding analysis 来报告，而不是因果论证。 The NLI ablation is underpowered with too few samples to draw meaningful conclusions, so I should keep the paper's discussion brief and note that while the bounding analysis suggests the gate doesn't artificially inflate results, we lack sufficient data to detect content-mediated effects. For the cross-axis gradient analysis, I'm setting up a qualitative comparison of NLI rejection rates against observed and corrected BT values across the three formality and verbosity dimensions, with expected performance metrics for each axis. The gradient pattern across axes suggests that amplification alone can't explain the results—if it were the only factor, all axes should show similar bias-to-text ratios, but the 20+ percentage point spread argues otherwise. There's a competing explanation where more invasive manipulations actually generate more artifacts rather than just detecting existing bias, though three data points aren't enough to rule this out definitively. The corrected register score dropping below 50% points to originality effects completely offsetting the marginal style signal after correction, which I should frame as a qualitative consistency check rather than a formal finding.\n\nNow I'm looking at the performance metrics across these three axes with their rejection rates, observed and corrected bias-to-text ratios, and the style effect sizes from the regression analysis.\n\nThe cross-axis gradient pattern is the strongest evidence against uniform amplification, even though three data points aren't enough for formal statistical testing—the consistent monotonic trend across axes does suggest the model detects axis-specific variation that simple amplification can't explain. The register axis is particularly interesting because after correction, the bias essentially disappears, which actually strengthens the argument that decomposing by axis reveals something meaningful rather than undermining it.\n\nI'm reconsidering how to structure this section since it's getting quite long.\n\nActually, I need to be more careful about the register placebo situation—exp-022 only tested formality and verbosity, not register itself. So for exp-024, I should use those formality/verbosity placebo values as a proxy for register's amplification, estimating around 8-9 percentage points, which would put the corrected register BT at roughly 47%. Let me verify I have predictions for all 11 planned experiments.\n\nAll eleven are accounted for. Now I should clarify whether exp-005 and exp-022 are actually distinct—exp-005 tests original versus double-rewrite across all judge and benchmark combinations, while exp-022 focuses on comparing original versus double-rewrite pairs in the same style direction. Now I'm verifying that all the statistical values fall within their expected ranges — the p-values, Bradley-Terry percentages, and Likert scores are all checking out as consistent with my predictions.\n\nEverything passes validation, so I can move forward with generating the output. I need to add a `result_rationale` field for each experiment that explains the underlying mechanism behind each result.\n\nBefore I write the YAML, let me check the project structure to see if there's an expected location or format for the predicted data file.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":30335,"cache_read_input_tokens":11447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":30335},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"510a4aa3-9bfc-4c61-ab4d-d3e433e97f95","uuid":"b6d5a39b-aa82-4886-92c7-32aa33e502c2"}
